"This directive allows results to be deferred during execution"
directive @defer(
    "Deferred behaviour is controlled by this argument"
    if: Boolean! = true,
    "A unique label that represents the fragment being deferred"
    label: String
  ) on FRAGMENT_SPREAD | INLINE_FRAGMENT

"Marks the field, argument, input field or enum value as deprecated"
directive @deprecated(
    "The reason for the deprecation"
    reason: String! = "No longer supported"
  ) on FIELD_DEFINITION | ARGUMENT_DEFINITION | ENUM_VALUE | INPUT_FIELD_DEFINITION

"This directive disables error propagation when a non nullable field returns null for the given operation."
directive @experimental_disableErrorPropagation on QUERY | MUTATION | SUBSCRIPTION

"Directs the executor to include this field or fragment only when the `if` argument is true"
directive @include(
    "Included when true."
    if: Boolean!
  ) on FIELD | FRAGMENT_SPREAD | INLINE_FRAGMENT

"Indicates an Input Object is a OneOf Input Object."
directive @oneOf on INPUT_OBJECT

"Directs the executor to skip this field or fragment when the `if` argument is true."
directive @skip(
    "Skipped when true."
    if: Boolean!
  ) on FIELD | FRAGMENT_SPREAD | INLINE_FRAGMENT

"Exposes a URL that specifies the behaviour of this scalar."
directive @specifiedBy(
    "The URL that specifies the behaviour of this scalar."
    url: String!
  ) on SCALAR

"""
 One source record rendered as an exploratory card — never a content
 copy. state is String DELIBERATELY: the lifecycle vocabulary is
 per-source-type heterogeneous (LOST_FOUND posts carry
 ACTIVE/RESOLVED/FOUND, events ACTIVE/CANCELLED/POSTPONED, jobs OPEN,
 listings ACTIVE/INACTIVE — and the urgent-alert cards carry none at
 all, measured from the assembler: the alert mapping passes null) — a
 single enum would lie about one shared vocabulary that does not
 exist; String is the honest type for the heterogeneous per-source
 state, exactly as the shared DiscoveryCardView carries it. updatedAt
 is the ISO-8601 instant (java.time.Instant.toString) — the GraphQL
 spec has no temporal scalar and this surface adds none (zero new
 dependencies on the locked Boot 4.1.1 → spring-graphql 2.0.5 →
 graphql-java 25.0 chain; a typed DateTime scalar stays a deliberate
 future decision behind a measured need, never a silent drift — the
 same discipline that keeps every version in this tree pinned).
 scopeLocationId is null for user-scoped sources; paid is null for
 organic (the disclosure contract: null never masquerades as either
 answer, AC-20-08); reason carries the human-explainable appearance
 reason (never empty on the FOR_YOU rail, AC-20-06/AC-20-11).
"""
type DiscoveryCard {
  paid: Boolean
  reason: String
  scopeLocationId: ID
  snippet: String
  sourceId: ID!
  sourceType: DiscoverySource!
  state: String
  title: String!
  updatedAt: String!
}

"""
 =====================================================================
 Phase 6 — the first composite journey's projection types (the
 corrected plan §11-Phase-6 + the inventory #599 §7 stage 4). Every
 type below is a BOUNDED READ PROJECTION of an owning module's
 contract (the plan's own words: "add bounded read projections") —
 the GraphQL surface owns no business rule (REST and GraphQL invoke
 the same use cases through the same declared SPIs, #599 §8).
 =====================================================================
 One discovery rail as rendered — the discovery projection's own
 contract (Wave D-1): the rail, its eligible cards (deduped by the
 (sourceType, sourceId) pair, bounded at the assembler's home card
 limit), and the source-side total so "عرض الكل" (the REST topic
 page, UJ-261) can state the real size before the user commits to
 navigating. totalEligible is a scoped per-rail count — Int's signed
 32-bit ceiling (2,147,483,647) is the documented bound, the exact
 priceCents house pattern.
"""
type DiscoveryRow {
  cards: [DiscoveryCard!]!
  row: DiscoveryRail!
  totalEligible: Int!
}

type Mutation {
  createService(input: ServiceInput!): Service!
  createStoreProduct(input: StoreProductCreateInput!): StoreProduct!
  createStoreProductVariant(input: StoreProductVariantCreateInput!, productId: ID!): StoreProductVariant!
  publishStoreProduct(id: ID!): StoreProduct!
  replaceStoreProductAttributes(id: ID!, values: [StoreProductAttributeValueInput!]!): [StoreProductAttributeValue!]!
  updateStoreProduct(id: ID!, input: StoreProductUpdateInput!): StoreProduct!
}

"""
 The feed node — the REST feed view's own field set (the same-journey
 comparison #599's gate demands: the home composite serves the SAME
 data the REST feed serves, nothing narrower, nothing wider). The
 author stays an opaque id (the identity seams own resolution —
 GraphQL never widens it); category/status/lostFoundState are the
 domain's closed vocabularies mirrored here as typed enums (the SDL
 is the typed boundary — the conversion lives in the surface's
 mapper, and the schema compatibility gate guards the values);
 lostFoundState rides ONLY LOST_FOUND posts (null for every other
 category — V171's own documented shape); reactionsCount is Int! with
 the documented 32-bit ceiling (a live grouped count, the priceCents
 pattern); media are the presigned display URLs (thumbUrl null until
 background processing has run — the L28 contract); the instants are
 ISO-8601 strings (the same deliberate no-new-scalar decision as
 DiscoveryCard.updatedAt).
"""
type NeighborhoodPost {
  authorId: ID!
  body: String!
  category: PostCategory!
  createdAt: String!
  declaredCommercial: Boolean!
  id: ID!
  locationId: ID!
  lostFoundState: LostFoundState
  media: [NeighborhoodPostMedia!]!
  reactedByMe: Boolean!
  reactionsCount: Int!
  status: PostStatus!
  title: String!
  updatedAt: String!
}

type NeighborhoodPostConnection {
  edges: [NeighborhoodPostEdge]
  pageInfo: PageInfo!
}

type NeighborhoodPostEdge {
  cursor: String!
  node: NeighborhoodPost!
}

"""
 One photo of a feed post as the home read carries it — the L48 media
 projection: a display URL pair and the position order; the object
 key, the status and every storage fact stay the media module's
 private concern. url is the time-limited presigned GET the media
 module computed for THIS read (always present); thumbUrl is null
 until background processing has run (clients fall back to url).
"""
type NeighborhoodPostMedia {
  contentType: String
  mediaId: ID!
  position: Int!
  thumbUrl: String
  url: String!
}

"""
 Relay PageInfo — Saleor's exact field set. Hand-declared once here
 for the UserCountableConnection (the runtime-generated connections
 declare the same field set, so one PageInfo serves both).
"""
type PageInfo {
  "When paginating forwards, the cursor to continue."
  endCursor: String
  "When paginating forwards, are there more items?"
  hasNextPage: Boolean!
  "When paginating backwards, are there more items?"
  hasPreviousPage: Boolean!
  "When paginating backwards, the cursor to continue."
  startCursor: String
}

"""
 =====================================================================
 Phase 7 — the composite post/source detail journey's comment
 projection (the corrected plan §11-Phase-7 + the inventory #599 §5
 item 3). The same bounded read projection discipline as every type
 above: the GraphQL surface owns no business rule — the REST comments
 read and the GraphQL comments leg invoke the SAME use case through
 the SAME declared SPI (#599 §8).
 =====================================================================
 One comment of a post as the detail journey carries it — the REST
 comments read view's own field set (the same-journey comparison the
 inventory's acceptance gate demands: nothing narrower, nothing
 wider). The author stays an opaque id (the identity seams own
 resolution — GraphQL never widens it); the comment never
 re-projects its post's own state (the comment is reached through the
 post gate); the instants are ISO-8601 strings (the same deliberate
 no-new-scalar decision as NeighborhoodPost.createdAt).
"""
type PostComment {
  authorId: ID!
  body: String!
  createdAt: String!
  id: ID!
  postId: ID!
  updatedAt: String!
}

type PostCommentConnection {
  edges: [PostCommentEdge]
  pageInfo: PageInfo!
}

type PostCommentEdge {
  cursor: String!
  node: PostComment!
}

type Query {
  """
   The caller's home discovery rails — the deterministic §1.4
   assembler's bounded projection (each rail ≤ 10 cards; the full
   filtered list lives on the REST topic page GET /discovery/rows/
   {row}, UJ-261). The membership IS the scope: no active membership
   answers the honest EMPTY list, never a widened one (AC-02-02); an
   ineligible/quiet rail is ABSENT entirely (AC-20-07 — the empty
   state is the client's honest rendering of absence, never a
   fabricated placeholder card). No arguments: the server owns
   eligibility, ordering and geo scope (#599 §8) — there is no
   client-side scope or filter axis on the home read.
  """
  discovery: [DiscoveryRow!]!
  """
   =================================================================
   Saleor Users domain — P1 wave (the canonical plan §5-P1: "retained
   Dashboard user/profile and permission operations using existing
   identity and console owners"). Root names, argument shapes and the
   connection envelope are the pinned Saleor 3.23.43 wire contract
   (saleor/graphql/schema.graphql, blob b8fa0dd8 — the measured roots
   me/user/staffUsers); the platform's identity owner remains the
   single source of truth behind them. The GLOBAL IDs on this surface
   are the typed Relay-style codec's (SaleorGlobalIdCodec): opaque
   base64("User:<uuid>") values — never raw UUIDs. The bounded User
   type closure and the permission mapping are recorded in the
   divergence registry; a declared-but-fake field would be the
   violation, not the absence.
   =================================================================
   "Return the currently authenticated user." — any authenticated
   caller's OWN profile read (the JWT subject through the identity
   owner). Nullable exactly as Saleor's: a subject with no users row
   (login before the REST /me bootstrap) answers null — the honest
   absence, never a fabricated profile.
  """
  me: User
  """
   The connection is NULLABLE deliberately (unlike services): the
   feed's owning use case answers an explicit denial — the REST
   feed's own 403 (no active membership, G-N3) — and on a NON-NULL
   root field a GraphQL field error propagates the null up to the
   root, destroying the WHOLE composite's data (discovery included —
   measured against the GraphQL spec's null propagation; strictly
   worse than the REST journey it replaces). The nullable envelope
   carries the owner's denial exactly the way the platform's error
   contract intends: the field resolves to null WITH one AUTHZ-
   classified error in errors[], while discovery still resolves its
   honest empty list — the composite never bends either owner's
   rules and never destroys the sibling section. The connection
   types (NeighborhoodPostConnection/Edge/PageInfo) are generated at
   startup by the same ConnectionTypeDefinitionConfigurer — never
   hand-declared here.
  """
  neighborhoodFeed(after: String, first: Int): NeighborhoodPostConnection
  """
   The id is NOT permission (the inventory §8: «لا يعتبر المعرّف
   إذنًا»): the owner re-validates the post's CURRENT state at open
   time (an unknown, hidden or deleted post answers the honest 404 —
   never trust the discovery card that pointed here) and the caller's
   active membership in the post's OWN neighborhood (403 otherwise).
   The field is NULLABLE deliberately (the neighborhoodFeed
   precedent): the denial resolves this field to null WITH one
   classified error in errors[] while the sibling comments field (or
   any other section of the same document) still resolves — the
   composite never destroys the sibling section and never bends the
   owner's rules. The answer is the feed node's own type — the
   same-journey field set (stored facts + caller-scoped reaction
   facts + presigned media), nothing narrower, nothing wider.
  """
  post(id: ID!): NeighborhoodPost
  """
   The post-detail journey's comments leg — the same bounded read the
   REST comments endpoint serves (GET /api/v1/posts/{postId}/comments):
   the post's comments on the owner's complete sort key createdAt ASC,
   id ASC (the owner pins the order — no shaky page boundaries),
   reached through the SAME post gate first (an unknown, hidden or
   deleted post answers the honest 404 — a hidden post's comments are
   absent exactly as the post itself is), then the membership gate in
   the post's OWN neighborhood (403; ANY verification state reads).
   The relay envelope is the Phase-1 connection contract the services
   and neighborhoodFeed fields established: forward-only first/after
   with the server's configured default page size and hard cap
   (marketplace.graphql.pagination.*) and no SDL literal default (the
   single-source rule). The connection is NULLABLE for the same
   field-level denial contract as post above. The connection types
   (PostCommentConnection/Edge/PageInfo) are generated at startup by
   the same ConnectionTypeDefinitionConfigurer — never hand-declared
   here.
  """
  postComments(after: String, first: Int, postId: ID!): PostCommentConnection
  service(id: ID!): Service
  """
   Forward-only keyset connection over ACTIVE catalog listings, ordered
   by createdAt DESC, id DESC. Cursors are opaque framework-generated
   positions and may be reused with a different first value because they
   identify a row's sort key, not an absolute offset. The configured
   default and hard maximum are owned by marketplace.graphql.pagination;
   do not add an SDL literal default that shadows that configuration.
  """
  services(after: String, first: Int): ServiceConnection!
  """
   "List of the shop's staff users." — the Saleor staff list with its
   countable-connection envelope (edges/pageInfo/totalCount, byte-shape
   from the pinned schema). FORWARD-ONLY keyset paging on this first
   slice: first/after ride the platform's ScrollPosition keyset
   contract (the storeProducts/services precedent — cursors identify a
   row's sort key (email|createdAt|updatedAt, id), never an absolute
   offset); before/last stay declared (the exact Saleor argument shape)
   but answer an explicit classified error until a backward scroll
   exists — a recorded, tested boundary, not a silent no-op. filter is
   likewise the declared wire shape with an explicit not-yet-supported
   error. Sort vocabulary is Saleor's full UserSortField enum; the
   fields with an owning indexed column (EMAIL/CREATED_AT/
   LAST_MODIFIED_AT) execute as real keyset sorts, the rest answer the
   same explicit error. MANAGE_STAFF on Saleor → ADMIN here.
  """
  staffUsers(
    "Return the elements in the list that come after the specified cursor."
    after: String,
    "Return the elements in the list that come before the specified cursor."
    before: String,
    "Filtering options for staff users."
    filter: StaffUserInput,
    "Retrieve the first n elements from the list."
    first: Int,
    "Retrieve the last n elements from the list."
    last: Int,
    "Sort staff users."
    sortBy: UserSortingInput
  ): UserCountableConnection
  """
   Dashboard's provider-owned catalog surface. This is a journey contract, not
   a mirror of the catalog tables; the fetchers delegate to ProductAdminSpi.
  """
  storeProduct(id: ID!): StoreProduct
  storeProductAttributeValues(productId: ID!): [StoreProductAttributeValue!]!
  storeProductTypes: [StoreProductType!]!
  storeProductVariantAttributeValues(variantId: ID!): [StoreProductAttributeValue!]!
  storeProductVariants(productId: ID!): [StoreProductVariant!]!
  """
   Forward-only keyset connection. Filter fields are limited to current domain
   attributes; the sort vocabulary is an allow-list backed by non-null indexed
   fields. Keep filter and sortBy identical when following an after cursor.
  """
  storeProducts(after: String, filter: StoreProductFilterInput, first: Int, sortBy: StoreProductSortInput): StoreProductConnection!
  """
   "Look up a user by ID or email address." — the Saleor wire shape
   carries id/email/externalReference; on this platform externalReference
   maps to the identity owner's subject (the OIDC subject IS the external
   account identifier of record — the same column the JWT sub joins).
   Requires one of MANAGE_STAFF/MANAGE_USERS/MANAGE_ORDERS on Saleor —
   enforced server-side as the platform's ADMIN capability at the
   identity use-case boundary (the RBAC mapping row of the divergence
   registry), never as a UI route guard.
  """
  user(email: String, externalReference: String, id: ID): User
}

type Service {
  category: String!
  currency: String!
  description: String
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  price: Float!
  status: ServiceStatus!
}

type ServiceConnection {
  edges: [ServiceEdge]
  pageInfo: PageInfo!
}

type ServiceEdge {
  cursor: String!
  node: Service!
}

"""
 The store catalog contract consumed by marketplace-admin. Monetary writes and
 reads use canonical decimal strings in minor units to preserve the full Java
 long precision; GraphQL Int is only signed 32-bit and Float is not exact money.
"""
type StoreProduct {
  currency: String!
  description: String
  id: ID!
  priceMinor: String!
  productTypeId: ID
  providerId: ID!
  status: StoreProductStatus!
  storeCategoryCode: String!
  title: String!
}

type StoreProductAttributeOption {
  code: String!
  id: ID!
  nameAr: String
  nameEn: String
  position: Int!
}

type StoreProductAttributeValue {
  assignmentId: ID!
  booleanValue: Boolean
  numberValue: String
  optionId: ID
  textValue: String
}

type StoreProductConnection {
  edges: [StoreProductEdge]
  pageInfo: PageInfo!
}

type StoreProductEdge {
  cursor: String!
  node: StoreProduct!
}

type StoreProductType {
  attributes: [StoreProductTypeAttribute!]!
  code: String!
  description: String
  id: ID!
  nameAr: String
  nameEn: String
}

type StoreProductTypeAttribute {
  assignmentId: ID!
  attributeDefinitionId: ID!
  code: String!
  nameAr: String
  nameEn: String
  options: [StoreProductAttributeOption!]!
  position: Int!
  required: Boolean!
  scope: StoreProductAttributeScope!
  valueType: StoreProductAttributeValueType!
}

type StoreProductVariant {
  currency: String!
  id: ID!
  name: String
  priceMinor: String!
  productId: ID!
  providerId: ID!
  reservedQuantity: Int!
  sku: String!
  status: StoreProductVariantStatus!
  stockQuantity: Int!
}

"""
 The bounded User projection — see the block comment above for the
 closure policy. id is the typed global ID; isStaff is the platform's
 staff concept (the ADMIN role); isActive is the honest login-identity
 fact the owner owns today (a pseudonymized account is not active);
 userPermissions is the server-enforced capability mapping
 (SaleorPermissionCatalog — the platform RBAC rendered in Saleor's
 permission vocabulary, the divergence-registry RBAC row).
"""
type User {
  "The date and time when the account was created (ISO-8601)."
  dateJoined: String
  "The email address of the user (null when the account has none)."
  email: String
  "The ID of the user — typed global ID (opaque, never a raw UUID)."
  id: ID!
  "Determine if the user is active (a live login identity exists)."
  isActive: Boolean!
  "Determine if the user is a staff admin (the platform ADMIN role)."
  isStaff: Boolean!
  "The account's effective server-side permissions."
  userPermissions: [UserPermission!]!
}

"""
 Saleor's connection envelope for countable user lists — declared
 (not runtime-generated) so the exact Saleor shape stands, including
 totalCount. Spring's ConnectionTypeDefinitionConfigurer skips
 already-declared connection types (measured in the 2.0.5 source —
 the getTypeOrNull filter), so this declaration wins and the generated
 connections elsewhere are untouched.
"""
type UserCountableConnection {
  edges: [UserCountableEdge!]!
  "Pagination data for this connection."
  pageInfo: PageInfo!
  "A total count of items in the collection."
  totalCount: Int
}

type UserCountableEdge {
  "A cursor for use in pagination (the row's keyset sort key)."
  cursor: String!
  "The item at the end of the edge."
  node: User!
}

"""
 Saleor's UserPermission shape (minus sourcePermissionGroups — the
 permission-group workbench is a later P1 slice with no owning model
 yet; the absence is recorded, not faked).
"""
type UserPermission {
  "Internal code for permission."
  code: PermissionEnum!
  "Describe action(s) allowed to do by permission."
  name: String!
}

"""
 The closed vocabulary of the home rails — the domain
 DiscoveryRowType mirrored (the render order §1.4 fixed: the enum's
 own numbering; URGENT_ALERTS above the ordinary rails, never ranked
 by popularity or a learned ordering, AC-20-06). The schema
 compatibility gate guards these values against unreviewed removals.
"""
enum DiscoveryRail {
  EVENTS_AND_OPPORTUNITIES
  FOLLOWED_SOURCES
  FOR_YOU
  LOST_FOUND
  NEIGHBORHOOD_RECOMMENDATIONS
  URGENT_ALERTS
}

"""
 The card's source-type vocabulary — the projection's closed five
 (a card NEVER carries a copy of its source: every card resolves
 back to its source record — the projection contract
 sourceId + sourceType + version/updatedAt, re-validated at open
 time by the owning module, AC-20-10).
"""
enum DiscoverySource {
  JOB
  NEIGHBORHOOD_EVENT
  NEIGHBORHOOD_POST
  PROVIDER_LISTING
  URGENT_ALERT
}

enum ErrorDetail {
  """
  The deadline expired before the operation could complete.
  
  For operations that change the state of the system, this error
  may be returned even if the operation has completed successfully.
  For example, a successful response from a server could have been
  delayed long enough for the deadline to expire.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 504 Gateway Timeout
  Error Type: UNAVAILABLE
  """
  DEADLINE_EXCEEDED
  """
  The server detected that the client is exhibiting a behavior that
  might be generating excessive load.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 420 Enhance Your Calm
  Error Type: UNAVAILABLE
  """
  ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM
  """
  The requested field is not found in the schema.
  
  This differs from `NOT_FOUND` in that `NOT_FOUND` should be used when a
  query is valid, but is unable to return a result (if, for example, a
  specific video id doesn't exist). `FIELD_NOT_FOUND` is intended to be
  returned by the server to signify that the requested field is not known to exist.
  This may be returned in lieu of failing the entire query.
  See also `PERMISSION_DENIED` for cases where the
  requested field is invalid only for the given user or class of users.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 404 Not Found
  Error Type: BAD_REQUEST
  """
  FIELD_NOT_FOUND
  """
  The client specified an invalid argument.
  
  Note that this differs from `FAILED_PRECONDITION`.
  `INVALID_ARGUMENT` indicates arguments that are problematic
  regardless of the state of the system (e.g., a malformed file name).
  
  HTTP Mapping: 400 Bad Request
  Error Type: BAD_REQUEST
  """
  INVALID_ARGUMENT
  """
  The provided cursor is not valid.
  
  The most common usage for this error is when a client is paginating
  through a list that uses stateful cursors. In that case, the provided
  cursor may be expired.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 404 Not Found
  Error Type: NOT_FOUND
  """
  INVALID_CURSOR
  """
  Unable to perform operation because a required resource is missing.
  
  Example: Client is attempting to refresh a list, but the specified
  list is expired. This requires an action by the client to get a new list.
  
  If the user is simply trying GET a resource that is not found,
  use the NOT_FOUND error type. FAILED_PRECONDITION.MISSING_RESOURCE
  is to be used particularly when the user is performing an operation
  that requires a particular resource to exist.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 400 Bad Request or 500 Internal Server Error
  Error Type: FAILED_PRECONDITION
  """
  MISSING_RESOURCE
  """
  Service Error.
  
  There is a problem with an upstream service.
  
  This may be returned if a gateway receives an unknown error from a service
  or if a service is unreachable.
  If a request times out which waiting on a response from a service,
  `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED` may be returned instead.
  If a service returns a more specific error Type, the specific error Type may
  be returned instead.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 502 Bad Gateway
  Error Type: UNAVAILABLE
  """
  SERVICE_ERROR
  """
  Request failed due to network errors.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 503 Unavailable
  Error Type: UNAVAILABLE
  """
  TCP_FAILURE
  """
  Request throttled based on server concurrency limits.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 503 Unavailable
  Error Type: UNAVAILABLE
  """
  THROTTLED_CONCURRENCY
  """
  Request throttled based on server CPU limits
  
  HTTP Mapping: 503 Unavailable.
  Error Type: UNAVAILABLE
  """
  THROTTLED_CPU
  """
  The server detected that the client is exhibiting a behavior that
  might be generating excessive load.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 429 Too Many Requests
  Error Type: UNAVAILABLE
  """
  TOO_MANY_REQUESTS
  """
  The operation is not implemented or is not currently supported/enabled.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 501 Not Implemented
  Error Type: BAD_REQUEST
  """
  UNIMPLEMENTED
  """
  Unknown error.
  
  This error should only be returned when no other error detail applies.
  If a client sees an unknown errorDetail, it will be interpreted as UNKNOWN.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 500 Internal Server Error
  """
  UNKNOWN
}

enum ErrorType {
  """
  Bad Request.
  
  There is a problem with the request.
  Retrying the same request is not likely to succeed.
  An example would be a query or argument that cannot be deserialized.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 400 Bad Request
  """
  BAD_REQUEST
  """
  The operation was rejected because the system is not in a state
  required for the operation's execution.  For example, the directory
  to be deleted is non-empty, an rmdir operation is applied to
  a non-directory, etc.
  
  Service implementers can use the following guidelines to decide
  between `FAILED_PRECONDITION` and `UNAVAILABLE`:
  
  - Use `UNAVAILABLE` if the client can retry just the failing call.
  - Use `FAILED_PRECONDITION` if the client should not retry until
  the system state has been explicitly fixed.  E.g., if an "rmdir"
       fails because the directory is non-empty, `FAILED_PRECONDITION`
  should be returned since the client should not retry unless
  the files are deleted from the directory.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 400 Bad Request or 500 Internal Server Error
  """
  FAILED_PRECONDITION
  """
  Internal error.
  
  An unexpected internal error was encountered. This means that some
  invariants expected by the underlying system have been broken.
  This error code is reserved for serious errors.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 500 Internal Server Error
  """
  INTERNAL
  """
  The requested entity was not found.
  
  This could apply to a resource that has never existed (e.g. bad resource id),
  or a resource that no longer exists (e.g. cache expired.)
  
  Note to server developers: if a request is denied for an entire class
  of users, such as gradual feature rollout or undocumented allowlist,
  `NOT_FOUND` may be used. If a request is denied for some users within
  a class of users, such as user-based access control, `PERMISSION_DENIED`
  must be used.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 404 Not Found
  """
  NOT_FOUND
  """
  The caller does not have permission to execute the specified
  operation.
  
  `PERMISSION_DENIED` must not be used for rejections
  caused by exhausting some resource or quota.
  `PERMISSION_DENIED` must not be used if the caller
  cannot be identified (use `UNAUTHENTICATED`
  instead for those errors).
  
  This error Type does not imply the
  request is valid or the requested entity exists or satisfies
  other pre-conditions.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 403 Forbidden
  """
  PERMISSION_DENIED
  """
  The request does not have valid authentication credentials.
  
  This is intended to be returned only for routes that require
  authentication.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 401 Unauthorized
  """
  UNAUTHENTICATED
  """
  Currently Unavailable.
  
  The service is currently unavailable.  This is most likely a
  transient condition, which can be corrected by retrying with
  a backoff.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 503 Unavailable
  """
  UNAVAILABLE
  """
  Unknown error.
  
  For example, this error may be returned when
  an error code received from another address space belongs to
  an error space that is not known in this address space.  Also
  errors raised by APIs that do not return enough error information
  may be converted to this error.
  
  If a client sees an unknown errorType, it will be interpreted as UNKNOWN.
  Unknown errors MUST NOT trigger any special behavior. These MAY be treated
  by an implementation as being equivalent to INTERNAL.
  
  When possible, a more specific error should be provided.
  
  HTTP Mapping: 520 Unknown Error
  """
  UNKNOWN
}

"""
 A LOST_FOUND post's lifecycle state — the domain LostFoundState
 mirrored (present only on LOST_FOUND posts; a resolved or recovered
 report never masquerades as an active one anywhere, JT-20).
"""
enum LostFoundState {
  ACTIVE
  FOUND
  RESOLVED
}

enum OrderDirection {
  "Specifies an ascending sort order."
  ASC
  "Specifies a descending sort order."
  DESC
}

"""
 The full Saleor 3.23.43 permission vocabulary — the closed wire enum
 the platform renders its RBAC capabilities into. Values the platform
 does not yet grant any role stay inert SDL vocabulary (never emitted);
 the granted subset is the tested SaleorPermissionCatalog mapping.
"""
enum PermissionEnum {
  HANDLE_CHECKOUTS
  HANDLE_PAYMENTS
  HANDLE_TAXES
  IMPERSONATE_USER
  MANAGE_APPS
  MANAGE_CHANNELS
  MANAGE_CHECKOUTS
  MANAGE_CUSTOMER_TYPES_AND_ATTRIBUTES
  MANAGE_DISCOUNTS
  MANAGE_GIFT_CARD
  MANAGE_MENUS
  MANAGE_OBSERVABILITY @deprecated(reason : "The observability feature is no longer supported. This permission will be removed in Saleor 3.24.")
  MANAGE_ORDERS
  MANAGE_ORDERS_IMPORT
  MANAGE_PAGES
  MANAGE_PAGE_TYPES_AND_ATTRIBUTES
  MANAGE_PLUGINS
  MANAGE_PRODUCTS
  MANAGE_PRODUCT_TYPES_AND_ATTRIBUTES
  MANAGE_SETTINGS
  MANAGE_SHIPPING
  MANAGE_STAFF
  MANAGE_TAXES
  MANAGE_TRANSLATIONS
  MANAGE_USERS
}

"""
 The feed post's category — the domain PostCategory mirrored (the
 feed's one filter axis on the REST side; the home read serves the
 whole feed, so this type carries no argument).
"""
enum PostCategory {
  CLASSIFIED
  GENERAL
  LOST_FOUND
  QUESTION
  RECOMMENDATION
  REQUEST
}

"""
 The feed post's moderation status — the domain PostStatus mirrored.
 The feed read answers VISIBLE only (a hidden post is absent — the
 honest 404/absence the REST feed already carries); the full
 vocabulary stays declared so the contract is the domain's, not a
 subset invented by the projection.
"""
enum PostStatus {
  HIDDEN_BY_MODERATOR
  VISIBLE
}

enum ServiceStatus {
  ACTIVE
  INACTIVE
}

enum StaffMemberStatus {
  "User account has been activated."
  ACTIVE
  "User account has not been activated yet."
  DEACTIVATED
}

enum StoreProductAttributeScope {
  PRODUCT
  VARIANT
  VARIANT_SELECTION
}

enum StoreProductAttributeValueType {
  BOOLEAN
  CHOICE
  NUMBER
  TEXT
}

enum StoreProductSortDirection {
  ASC
  DESC
}

enum StoreProductSortField {
  CREATED_AT
  PRICE_MINOR
  STATUS
  TITLE
}

enum StoreProductStatus {
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
  DRAFT
  SUSPENDED
}

enum StoreProductVariantStatus {
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
  SUSPENDED
}

enum UserSortField {
  "Sort users by created at."
  CREATED_AT
  "Sort users by email."
  EMAIL
  "Sort users by first name."
  FIRST_NAME
  "Sort users by last modified at."
  LAST_MODIFIED_AT
  "Sort users by last name."
  LAST_NAME
  "Sort users by order count."
  ORDER_COUNT
  "Sort users by rank. Note: This option is available only with the `search` filter."
  RANK
}

"""
 Input speaks integer cents — the domain money language of every write
 surface (REST CreateListingRequest.priceCents, V2 price_cents >= 0,
 BookingInfo). DGS's DefaultInputObjectMapper binds input keys to Java
 record components BY NAME, so the field name must equal the
 ServiceInput.priceCents record component (the same by-name contract the
 spring-graphql binder enforced before the Phase-2 migration). (The read
 side keeps display units: Service.price — major units via
 ServiceMapper.) Int is a signed 32-bit scalar per the GraphQL spec —
 the explicit shared ceiling 2,147,483,647 is declared in ServiceInput
 via @Max and enforced by the fetcher's explicit Bean Validation.
"""
input ServiceInput {
  category: String!
  description: String
  name: String!
  priceCents: Int!
}

"""
 Saleor's staff-user filter input — the declared wire shape of the
 filter argument (see staffUsers: an explicit classified error until
 the owning query capability exists).
"""
input StaffUserInput {
  ids: [ID!]
  search: String
  status: StaffMemberStatus
}

input StoreProductAttributeValueInput {
  assignmentId: ID!
  booleanValue: Boolean
  numberValue: String
  optionId: ID
  textValue: String
}

"""
 priceMinor must be a non-negative, base-10 integer string (for example "149900").
 Domain services remain authoritative for ownership, ProductType rules and publishability.
"""
input StoreProductCreateInput {
  currency: String!
  description: String
  priceMinor: String!
  productTypeId: ID
  storeCategoryCode: String!
  title: String!
}

"""
 Adapted to the catalog fields the platform actually owns. Prices are minor-unit
 decimal strings to avoid GraphQL Int/Float precision loss. Bounds and PRICE_MINOR
 sorting require currency because priceMinor values across currencies are incomparable.
"""
input StoreProductFilterInput {
  currency: String
  maxPriceMinor: String
  minPriceMinor: String
  productTypeId: ID
  search: String
  status: StoreProductStatus
  storeCategoryCode: String
}

input StoreProductSortInput {
  direction: StoreProductSortDirection! = DESC
  field: StoreProductSortField! = CREATED_AT
}

input StoreProductUpdateInput {
  description: String
  priceMinor: String!
  title: String!
}

input StoreProductVariantCreateInput {
  attributeValues: [StoreProductAttributeValueInput!]!
  name: String
  priceMinor: String!
  sku: String!
}

"""
 Saleor's user sorting input and field vocabulary (full closure; the
 subset with an owning column executes — EMAIL/CREATED_AT/
 LAST_MODIFIED_AT — the rest answer the explicit not-supported error).
"""
input UserSortingInput {
  "Specifies the direction in which to sort users."
  direction: OrderDirection!
  "Sort users by the selected field."
  field: UserSortField!
}
